                <head>ETA–38. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On July 11, 1960, during his news conference at Newport, Rhode Island, at which
                    Secretary Herter was present, President Eisenhower commented on the basic ideas
                    that would guide United States participation in the forthcoming meetings of the
                    Committee of 21 at Bogotá, Colombia. He announced, <hi rendition="#italic">inter
                        alia</hi>, that the United States was committed to cooperative action aimed
                    at helping Latin American governments to achieve “soundly based” social and
                    economic progress, accelerate the trend already evident toward greater respect
                    for human rights and principles of democratic government, and improve the
                    opportunities of the bulk of the population in Latin America “to share in and
                    contribute to an expanding national product.” For the full text of the
                    President’s statement, see <hi rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the
                        Presidents, Eisenhower</hi>, 1960–61, pp. 568–575.</p>
            </div>
